{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And after it there shall not be like it.</b> In a single species of locust — for that of Joel comprised many species, and the other species were larger than the species of ארבה; but here in Egypt there were also many species. And David came and explained (Psalms 105): ״אמר ויבא ארבה וילק ואין מספר״, and it is written (ibid. 78): ״ויתן לחסיל יבולם ויגיעם לארבה״ — and there are seven species of locust, and these are: ארבה, סלעם, חרגול, חגב, גזם, ילק, and חסיל, and all of them were in Egypt. And this is why you find in this pericah the word ארבה seven times, and this species of ארבה was more numerous than all the other species, and on account of this the verse says ״ואחריו לא יהיה כן״ — that is, in a single species of locust. And therefore its name is called ארבה, because its swarm (ארבו) is more multitudinous than the other species, as it is written of the locust (Joel 2): ״חילי הגדול״." "<b>And the Ramban</b>, may his memory be for a blessing, explained: since the plague of the locust comes in the way of the world and the course of nature, and moreover it came by means of an east wind as is customary, therefore the verse testified: ״לפניו לא היה כן ארבה כמוהו ואחריו לא יהיה כן״ — in the natural course of the world." "<b>And Rabbenu</b> Hananel, may his memory be for a blessing, explained ״ואחריו לא יהיה כן״ — by the hand of a prophet. And its parallel is what is written of Hezekiah (II Kings 18:5): ״בה' אלהי ישראל בטח ואחריו לא היה כמוהו״ — the meaning of this is: in trust (bitaḥon) there was none like him, but in fear of sin and in other traits there was one like him. And likewise what is written of Joshua (Joshua 10:14): ״ולא היה כיום ההוא לפניו ואחריו לשמוע ה' בקול איש״ — that is, to make the sun stand still through the speech of a man; but the Holy One, blessed be He, exalted be His great name, made the sun stand still for Moses our Master and for Nakdimon ben Gurion of Himself, without the speech of a man."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The wordplay links ארבה to a root meaning 'multiply/swarm'; translated the allusion literally without expanding. Citations normalized to standard chapter:verse; Psalms 105 and 78 have no verse numbers specified in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:39.755880804Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}